<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#kongo-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kongo language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kikongo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a>, or Kikongo, is a Bantu language spoken by the Kongo people in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>. It was the language of the Kingdom of Kongo and its coastal neighbours Loango, Kakongo and Ngoyo. A Catholic catechism printed in 1624 is among the earliest books in any Bantu language, and the trade language Kituba developed from it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kongo-language.jpg" alt="Kongo language" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rasulo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LanguageMap-Kikongo-Kituba.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The people, Bakongo speaking a northern dialect of <b>Kikongo</b>, included the Vili, the Yombe, Kunyi, Lumbu and Babongo Pygmies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-loango">Kingdom of Loango · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They put it into <b>Kikongo</b> words, calling the Bible the nkanda ukisi, the holy book, and a church the nzo a ukisi, the holy house.</blockquote><a href="/societies/kongo">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Kongo</a></li><li><blockquote>Kakongo was one of the <b>Kikongo</b>-speaking kingdoms north of the Congo estuary that recognised the senior Kingdom of Loango in early times.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kakongo">Kingdom of Kakongo</a></li><li><blockquote>Ngoyo was one of three <b>Kikongo</b>-speaking kingdoms on the coast north of the Congo estuary, alongside the Kingdom of Loango and the Kingdom of Kakongo.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ngoyo">Kingdom of Ngoyo</a></li><li><blockquote>Like neighbouring <b>Kikongo</b>-speaking polities it was probably ruled by a sacred king drawn from leading lineages, with authority tied to ancestral and territorial spirits.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-vungu">Kingdom of Vungu</a></li></ul></section>
